- op: create  # a326
  at: '2026-08-26T00:50:10Z'  # a326
  by: agent  # a326
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a326
  clock: 1  # a326
  nonce: a326  # a326
  data:  # a326
    kind: invariant  # a326
    title: "Taint credited across an external-resource boundary (open handle, fetched bytes, socket fd, out-of-repo callee)"  # a326
    status: violated  # a326
    priority: 1  # a326
    parent:  # a326
    tags:  # a326
    - taint  # a326
    - measurement_0006  # a326
    isbefore: []  # a326
    duplicate_of: []  # a326
    not_duplicate_of: []  # a326
    pr_ref:  # a326
    description: "Found by the 0006 adversarial refutation pass. This family produced 5 of the 6 uncontested refutations (jaeger#0, ArkLib#0, ArkLib#1, guacamole-client#4, tmux#3) out of 44 TPs attacked.\n\nThe refuters drew the correct line, which is narrower than \"receivers never count\": a receiver built by IN-PROGRAM computation is a genuine true positive (ArkLib#4, path.with_name(path.name + \".bak\"), SURVIVED), while a handle returned by open() is not, because no line in the repository carries the path into it. Measurement 0001 rubric already names this: \"taint flows through in-program computation, not through an external resource selected by the tainted value.\"\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE: removing this family moves 0006 from 39.3% to 33.9% per situation. It was PRE-REGISTERED before the refutation pass with predicted arithmetic (39.3 -> 36.6 for shape (a) alone) and withheld from the refuters, who then found it independently.\n\nShape (d) additionally means a hop can be attributed to source the analyzer never read."  # a326
    fields:  # a326
      statement: "Taint is credited across an external-resource boundary in four distinct shapes, each of which makes the tainted value a SELECTOR of the resource rather than a value computed into the sink argument: (a) the handle returned by open(<tainted path>) is treated as a taint propagator and its .write reported as the sink; (b) bytes fetched by a command whose ARGUMENT is tainted (curl \"$URL\" > file) are credited to the redirect; (c) a socket fd is credited to a sink fed by recvmsg bytes read through it; (d) an out-of-repo callee (Erlang ct:pal) is summarized as the in-repo sink it resembles. Shape (a) is inconsistent with the tool itself: for structurally near-identical statements it elsewhere names open @ builtins as the sink with the tainted path as ARGUMENT, which is correct."  # a326
      root_cause: "The propagator treats a call whose ARGUMENT is tainted as producing a tainted RETURN, with no notion of a resource-selection barrier, and summarizes callees it cannot see as their nearest in-repo analogue."  # a326
- op: discuss  # 6ed0
  at: '2026-08-26T07:51:50Z'  # 6ed0
  by: agent  # 6ed0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6ed0
  clock: 2  # 6ed0
  nonce: 6ed0  # 6ed0
  message: "SHAPE (a) MECHANISM FIXED; MEASURED INERT ON THE COHORT. Both halves matter and\nthe second is the more important one.\n\nTHE FIX. A definition no longer inherits taint across an I/O boundary: an I/O\nprimitive's return comes from the OTHER SIDE of it -- a handle on a resource the\nargument merely NAMED, or bytes the argument merely ADDRESSED -- so it is not a\ncomputation on that argument. That is this item's root_cause (\"treats a call\nwhose ARGUMENT is tainted as producing a tainted RETURN, with no notion of a\nresource-selection barrier\") answered directly, and it is the 0001 rubric's own\ntie-break made executable. DERIVED FROM THE CATALOGUE, not a curated list of\n\"opening\" calls.\n\nMEASURED, control in the same run:\n  tainted PATH, constant payload        fixed path, tainted PAYLOAD\n  out = open(args.outfile,\"w\")          out = open(\"/tmp/fixed.txt\",\"w\")\n  out.write(\"a constant banner\")        out.write(args.payload)\n  before: open ddg + file.write ddg     before: file.write ddg\n  after:  open ddg + file.write ddg_MIXED   after: file.write ddg\nThe tool's internal inconsistency this item names is confirmed: it already named\n`open` correctly and ADDITIONALLY credited the handle.\n\nINERT ON THE 0006 COHORT -- byte-identical across all 11 repositories that carry\na ddg_mixed finding. Two independent reasons, both structural:\n\n  1. Section 3a is CONFIRM-ONLY. The walk raises confidence and never removes a\n     flow, so what this withdraws is the false claim of PRECISION (INV-sadah),\n     not the finding. Inclusion still rests on call-graph reachability -- that is\n     INV-karud, not this item.\n  2. The cohort's instances are UNREACHABLE BY ANY CATALOGUE RULE. jaeger#0 is\n     `with open(args.output,'w') as f: f.write(summary)` where `summary` came\n     from `parse_diff_file(args.diff)`: the external read happens INSIDE A\n     FIRST-PARTY CALLEE, so no row in any catalogue describes the crossing.\n\nSTATUS STAYS violated. Shapes (b), (c) and (d) are untouched:\n  (b) bytes fetched by a command whose ARGUMENT is tainted (curl \"$URL\" > file)\n      -- the crossing is a subprocess the analyzer models as opaque.\n  (c) a socket fd credited to a sink fed by recvmsg bytes read through it -- the\n      C arm, where the flow is structural/reachability-only.\n  (d) an out-of-repo callee (Erlang ct:pal) summarized as its nearest in-repo\n      analogue -- a summary-provenance question, not a propagation one.\nAnd shape (a) survives whenever the read is wrapped in a first-party function,\nwhich the cohort says is the common case.\n\nCONVERGENCE WORTH RECORDING FOR THE NEXT RE-RANK: three independent lines of work\nnow terminate at ADR-0017 SECTION 4 FUNCTION SUMMARIES -- section 7a's\naddressable domain (measurement 0007: zero), the walk's reach (76.3% of blocked\nwalks are cross_function), and this precision rule (inert because the crossing\nhides inside a callee). That is a plan-level input, not a decision I am taking\nunilaterally."  # 6ed0
