- op: discuss  # 784c
  at: '2026-08-28T16:27:00Z'  # 784c
  by: agent  # 784c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 784c
  clock: 4  # 784c
  nonce: 784c  # 784c
  message: "SHAPE (c) IS ELIMINATED, MEASURED ON THIS ITEM'S OWN NAMED INSTANCE. Not by a\npropagator change -- by removing the row that made the fd a source. INV-nular\nF2 (WI-dosov, PR #602) took `socket`/`bind`/`listen` out of net_recv in ten\nlanguages, C among them.\n\nA/B on tmux (this item's tmux#3 repo) and tmate, arms proven live by a canary on\nwhether c's `socket` still carries a net_recv row (OLD PRESENT / NEW ABSENT):\n\n  tmux    UNTRUSTED-NO-HOST-FS  violated(2)  -> inconclusive(0)\n          both OLD rows sourced from `socket`\n  tmate   UNTRUSTED-NO-HOST-FS  violated(2)  -> inconclusive(0)\n          both OLD rows sourced from `socket`\n  CONTROL HOSTSECRET-NO-HOST-FS unchanged in both repos -- tmux 5 rows\n          (environ x2, getenv x3), tmate 4 rows (getenv x4)\n\nThat is exactly the shape as stated: \"a socket fd is credited to a sink fed by\nrecvmsg bytes read through it\". The fd stops being a source; `recv`/`recvfrom`/\n`recvmsg` are KEPT, so the actual receive still is one. The false half went and\nthe true half stayed.\n\nWHY THIS ITEM DID NOT PREDICT THE FIX. The root_cause is written about the\nPROPAGATOR (\"treats a call whose ARGUMENT is tainted as producing a tainted\nRETURN, with no notion of a resource-selection barrier\"). For shape (c) that\ndiagnosis was one level too deep: nothing had to reason about resource\nselection, because `socket()` should never have been a taint SOURCE in the\nfirst place. A catalogue row was manufacturing the value the propagator then\ncarried.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING. I did not map the 0006 finding IDs onto these evidence\nrows, so I cannot say \"tmux#3 specifically is gone\" -- only that the sole\nuntrusted_input flows in tmux and tmate were socket-sourced and are now absent.\nAnd the shape can still RECUR through `accept`, which I kept deliberately (it\nreturns the peer address, which is attacker-chosen, so it is a real source);\nif a future refutation finds an accept-fd credited to a recvmsg-fed sink, that\nis shape (c) again by another door.\n\nSTATUS STAYS violated. (a) mechanism shipped (#544) and measured INERT -- the\ncrossing happens inside a first-party callee. (b) and (d) untouched. The\nconvergence on ADR-0017 s4 function summaries (WI-famig) is unchanged."  # 784c
- op: discuss  # 68b7
  at: '2026-08-31T19:16:27Z'  # 68b7
  by: agent  # 68b7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 68b7
  clock: 5  # 68b7
  nonce: 68b7  # 68b7
  message: "SHAPE (b) REPRODUCES, AND THE CHEAP FIX IS REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT. No code\nshipped; the deliverable is the negative result and the prerequisite it names.\n\nWHAT THE RULING ALREADY SAYS. io_primitives/bash.yaml's header enumerates two\ncases and rules them: \"curl -o file -> write performed by an OPAQUE EXTERNAL\nPROGRAM -> stays subprocess + opacity, NOT catalogued as fs_write\" versus\n\"echo x > file -> write performed by THE SHELL ITSELF -> catalogued here\".\n`curl ... > file` is a THIRD case the header never enumerated, and bash.py's\nfile_redirect branch extended the second row to cover it (\"even for an\nexternal command the redirection is established before exec\"). That extension\nis where the shape lives: the shell does open and truncate the target, but the\nBYTES are the child's. One fs_write tag carries two different assertions --\n\"the shell created/truncated this file\" (true) and \"this program's data\ncrosses out here\" (false) -- and AUTO_SINK_ZONE_MAP consumes the second.\n\nLIVE REPRO, cold, item's own instance. guacamole-docker/build.d/\n020-download-drivers.sh download_driver/3 carries both forms: :69\n`curl -L \"$URL\" | tar -xz ... > \"$DEST_PATH/$DEST_JAR\"` and :71\n`curl -L \"$URL\" > \"$DEST_PATH/$DEST_JAR\"`. HOSTSECRET-NO-HOST-FS = violated(6),\n3 of them redirect-sinks in that file. A two-file minimal repro (curl-redirect\nvs `echo \"version is $MSG\" > f`) returns findings that are BYTE-IDENTICAL in\nshape -- same env.environ source at FILE granularity, same redirect.> sink,\nsame structural/unavailable walk. bash is dataflow_capable=false with four\nblockers (cfg_mapping, atomic_statement, def_use_extractor, ddg_spec), so\ninclusion is call_graph_reachability alone.\n\nEMISSION SHAPE FIRST (rule: measure before building). 576 write redirects over\n15 cohort repos: external byte-producer 244 (42.4%), builtin 145 (25.2%),\nshell_function 5, rest non-command bodies. FINDING level, 37 redirect-sink\nfindings over 9 repos: ALL_EXTERNAL 10, has_shell_write 23, mixed 4.\n\nTHE GATE I WAS GOING TO BUILD, AND WHY IT IS WRONG. \"Byte-producer is an\nopaque external program => the redirect is not a taint sink for shell-scope\nvalues\" (shipped as an ADR-0049 deferred-crossing boundary shadowing fs_write,\nwhich is the machinery DEFERRED_CROSSING_SHADOWS already provides). Reading\nall 10 ALL_EXTERNAL rows back against source refutes it:\n\n  beads scripts/sign-windows.sh:99\n    echo \"$WINDOWS_SIGNING_CERT_PFX_BASE64\" | base64 -d > \"$CERT_FILE\"\n    Last pipeline stage is external (base64), so the gate fires -- but stage\n    one is a builtin echo carrying a secret that is never assigned in-file\n    (documented at :8/:79 as a GitHub secret). A signing certificate really is\n    decoded onto disk. The gate DELETES A TRUE POSITIVE.\n\n  cilium images/builder/update-cilium-builder-image.sh:22 and :28\n    sed -E \"s#...${image}:.*$#\\1${image_full}#\" \"${i}\" > \"${i}.sedtmp\"\n    An in-process value IS interpolated into the written bytes, so the gate's\n    premise (\"external producer => the shell contributes nothing\") is false as\n    a MECHANISM claim. NOT CLAIMED: that this finding is a true positive on\n    host_secret -- image_full is ${1}, a positional parameter, not an env read.\n\nNarrowing to network-fetch producers only (curl/wget) survives both\ncounterexamples and covers :69 and :71, but ADR-0016 deliberately keeps curl's\nnetwork semantics OUT of the bash catalogue, so that gate is a curated list,\nnot a catalogue derivation. Adding a \"target carries an expansion => keep\"\nescape hatch to make it conservative loses the item's OWN instance, because\n\"$DEST_PATH/$DEST_JAR\" is an expansion.\n\nWHY NO CHEAP GATE EXISTS -- the decisive one. bash.py's env-read rule is \"an\nexpansion whose name is never assigned in this file\". In 020-download-drivers.sh\nDEST_PATH=\"$DESTINATION/drivers/\" and $DESTINATION is never assigned in that\nfile, so by the analyzer's own rule the redirect TARGET is env-derived and the\nfinding is correct BY ITS OWN LIGHTS. It is false only because DESTINATION is\nassigned at guacamole-docker/bin/build-guacamole.sh:58 (DESTINATION=\"$2\") in\nthe script that SOURCES this one. Refuting the item's named instance therefore\nrequires name-level flow ACROSS a `source` edge. Every cheaper gate either\ndeletes a measured true positive or leaves the named instance standing.\n\nSTATUS STAYS violated. Shape (b) is not fixable at bash's current granularity;\nthe prerequisite is filed rather than bolted on here."  # 68b7
- op: discuss  # 4a48
  at: '2026-08-31T19:30:53Z'  # 4a48
  by: agent  # 4a48
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4a48
  clock: 6  # 4a48
  nonce: 4a48  # 4a48
  message: "SHAPE (d) RULING: THE NAMED INSTANCE IS DEAD; THE STATED MECHANISM IS\nUNSUPPORTED AND NOW UNTESTABLE AT ITS OWN INSTANCE. Shape (d) should not hold\nthis item open. INV-fumod stays violated on shape (b) alone.\n\nTHE INSTANCE. rabbitmq#5 anchored configure_ct_peers_environment/1\n(deps/rabbitmq_ct_helpers/src/rabbit_ct_vm_helpers.erl:847-859) and named sink\nio_lib:format. That source is unchanged on disk -- os:getenv(\"DEPS_DIR\") at\n:851 flowing only to ct:pal/3 at :853-854 and rpc_all/4 at :857 -- so the\ninput to the test is the same one the refuter judged.\n\nLIVE, host-secret-no-logging, two independent looks, both cold:\n  whole repo   violated(138), all env_read. Sink primitives over the 100 rows\n               the list carries: logger.error 77, logger.debug 73,\n               logger.warning 70, io.format 55, logger.info 47, io.puts 1.\n               NO io_lib SINK ANYWHERE IN THE OUTPUT. No listed row is sourced\n               under deps/rabbitmq_ct_helpers.\n  subtree      deps/rabbitmq_ct_helpers alone: INCONCLUSIVE(0).\n               POSITIVE CONTROL, because a gate that never fires proves\n               nothing: the erlang catalogue is live in that run (84 sources,\n               151 sinks) and 29 taint findings are computed there -- the run\n               had the power to report this flow and did not.\n  DISCLOSED: the whole-repo evidence LIST is capped at 100 of 138\n  (_MAX_EVIDENCE_ROWS); the subtree run is what closes that gap for this\n  instance, which is why it was run rather than inferred.\n\nWHY IT WENT, AND IT IS NOT THIS ITEM'S FIX. INV-nular F6 deleted the io_lib\nrows from io_primitives/erlang.yaml (io_lib:format returns an iolist and\nwrites nothing). The sink this instance named ceased to exist. Nothing about\nsummary provenance changed.\n\nTHE MECHANISM AS STATED IS NOT CONFIRMED. \"An out-of-repo callee is summarized\nas the in-repo sink it resembles\" does not reproduce:\n  1. Minimal discriminating repro: a module calling os:getenv then ct:pal/2\n     produces NO finding, while a control calling io:format on the same value\n     produces one. The unresolved remote call becomes a synthetic external\n     node erlang:ct:0-0:pal:function that matches no catalogue row.\n  2. By construction: erlang.py resolves a remote call as\n     NameResolver.lookup(\"ct:pal\"), and NameResolver's suffix index preserves\n     the original separators, so \"ct:pal\" can only match a registry key ending\n     in \":ct:pal\". rabbitmq has no ct module on disk, so no key can.\n  3. The live instance is silent, above.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING. I cannot reconstruct the route the 0006 run actually\ntook to io_lib:format, because the row is gone; a reachability route through\nsome other io_lib:format caller is at least as consistent with the refuter's\ntext (\"no io_lib:format call anywhere in the repository takes a value derived\nfrom :851\") as summarization is. So I am NOT asserting the tool never\nsummarized ct:pal -- I am asserting the claim was never demonstrated, does not\nreproduce by three independent checks, and can no longer be tested at its own\ninstance. Filing a fresh item against an unreproducible mechanism would be\nchasing; if the shape recurs it will arrive with a live instance attached."  # 4a48
